(borge-bug (id "BORGE-1790987435")
  (title "transaction left open (BEGIN + statement, no COMMIT/ROLLBACK) on the request cancellation/disconnect path in Store_db.with_transaction")
  (status open)
  (created 2026-10-02)
  (filed-by roerick)
  (doc (|FOUND 2026-10-02 while diagnosing the prod lock pileup
(see BORGE-1790987430). The leaked transaction that took the tree down came
from THIS code path.

image/lib/store_db.ml with_transaction / with_dynamic_transaction open a real
pgx transaction (BEGIN ... COMMIT/ROLLBACK) and rely on Fun.protect to run the
commit/rollback:

    Io_eio.Pg.with_transaction ~isolation pg (fun tx ->
        t.current_tx <- Some (tx, mu);
        set_local_pp_user tx;
        Fun.protect
          ~finally:(fun () -> t.current_tx <- None)
          (fun () -> f (Some (tx, mu))))

Two problems compound:

1) `t.current_tx <- None` in the finally only clears the OCaml-side field. The
   pgx transaction object owns the wire BEGIN. pgx's with_transaction itself
   has a catch-arm that rolls back on an exception, BUT under Eio a fiber can
   be CANCELLED (client disconnect / nginx 499 / request switch teardown). The
   pgx catch is `catch (fun () -> f conn >>= ... commit) (fun e -> rollback)`,
   built on its Io monad; Eio cancellation is not an ordinary OCaml exception
   raised at that point, so the rollback can be skipped and the connection is
   released from the mutex with the server-side transaction still open. The
   next request reuses the same pgx connection and inherits an open tx.

2) There is no owner on a per-connection basis: the pgx `conn.in_transaction`
   flag lives in the connection, but Store_db's `current_tx` is store-global
   and is the only thing readers consult. Once cleared by the lost path, the
   connection is handed out as if idle while the server still holds the tx.

This is the documented hazard of riding/opening transactions across an
Eio-cancellation boundary: `with_eio_conn_lock` uses Cancel.protect around the
SQL section, but the BEGIN/f body/COMMIT sequence spans fiber-suspending work
(gc, DB round trips) where cancellation can land.

FIX DIRECTION:
  - Wrap the transaction lifetime so Eio cancellation is converted to a
    rollback: match on Eio.Cancel.Cancelled, issue ROLLBACK, re-raise; or use
    Eio.Switch.on_release / a protect that always closes the pgx tx.
  - Strengthen the safety net: on connection reuse, if pgx reports
    in_transaction (or a cheap `SHOW transaction_isolation` / `SELECT 1` probe
    is in an aborted/blocked state), roll back before handing the conn out.
  - Pair with the DB-level idle_in_transaction_session_timeout
    (BORGE-1790987430) as defense in depth.
Add a test that cancels a fiber mid-transaction and asserts the subsequent use
of the same store issues no open tx (and that pg_stat_activity shows none).
|))
)
